what we're trying to do in the  laboratory is to simulate a process that  we think was very common on the early  earth and it's still common today in  fact whatever rain falls on a volcano it  makes little puddles that are heated by  the volcanic energy when it stops  raining those puddles evaporates so we  have a wet/dry cycle occurring all the  time  yelling volcanic areas of the  present-day earth in the laboratory we  are simulating that by building a  chamber the chamber has got to be  anaerobic being there's can't be any air  in it because the oxygen in today's  chamber and in today's atmosphere will  damage the chemicals that we're trying  to work with so we are loading this  chamber up with a inert gas that happens  to be carbon dioxide but we could use  something like nitrogen if we wanted to  carbon dioxide is easy to use we have a  tank sort of blowing co2 into the  chamber and as the co2 comes into  contact with a reaction medium that we  are testing it dries it out we have the  equivalent of volcanic heat we have two  heaters under a big disc an aluminium  disc that is turning very slowly and  every vial that is embedded in this disc  and there's 24 of them goes through one  wet/dry cycle every 90 minutes so that's  what how we're simulating it we're  drying it out under a stream of carbon  dioxide where rehydrating was just plain  old water then it goes through another  drying cycle this occurs every 90  minutes and in a typical experiment  we'll have about four of these cycles in  a day that we will then analyze the  result and see what we've made if  anything
 

